sudo yum install java-11-openjdk-devel


If Java 11 OpenJDK is missing from your system repositories, you can install it manually by downloading the binary archives directly from official sources like Java.net or Oracle. 
1. Download the OpenJDK 11 Binary 
Go to the Archived OpenJDK GA Releases and download the .tar.gz file for Linux/x64. You can also use wget if you have the direct link: 
bash

wget https://download.java.net/java/GA/jdk11/13/GPL/openjdk-11.0.1_linux-x64_bin.tar.gz

2. Extract the Archive
Choose a directory for your Java installation, such as /usr/local/ or a custom jdks folder in your home directory. 
bash

# Create directory
sudo mkdir -p /usr/lib/jvm

# Extract to the directory
sudo tar -xzf openjdk-11.0.1_linux-x64_bin.tar.gz -C /usr/lib/jvm

3. Configure Environment Variables 
You must tell your system where to find the new Java binaries by updating your JAVA_HOME and PATH. 

    Open your shell profile file (e.g., ~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc) in a text editor:
    bash

    nano ~/.bashrc

    Add the following lines at the end of the file (adjust the folder name if yours is different):
    bash

    export JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/jdk-11.0.1
    export PATH=$JAVA_HOME/bin:$PATH

    Save and exit, then reload the profile:
    bash

    source ~/.bashrc

     

4. Verify the Installation
Check that the system recognizes the manual installation: 
bash

java -version

It should display "openjdk version 11.x.x".
Alternative: Use SDKMAN! 
If you prefer a tool to manage multiple Java versions without manual configuration, use SDKMAN!. It allows you to install Java 11 even if it's missing from your OS repo: 
bash

sdk install java 11.0.x-open
